# Equally Likely Combinations

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 11.

=== layout: stacked-panels


@top

![Slide image 9](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/image_009.png)

- How many different ways can we get 4 boys and 3 girls? 35 ways!!!

@bottom

![Slide image 10](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/image_010.svg)

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 12.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 10](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/image_009.png)

- How many different ways can we get 4 boys and 3 girls?
- 35 ways out of 128 (2^7^) possible
- $\rightarrow \frac{35}{128} = 0.2734375 \approx 27.3\%$
- 4 girls and 3 boys the same 27.34%, these are mutually exclusive so&#8230;
- 4 boys and 3 girls or 4 girls and 3 boys is
- $27.3\% + 27.3\% \approx 54.7\%$

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 13.

=== layout: two-over-one-panels

@top-left

![Slide image 11](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/image_011.png)

@top-right

- $\text{(a)}\quad\frac{1}{2^6} = \frac{1}{64} = 1.6\%$
- $\text{(b)}\quad\frac{6}{64} = 9.4\%$
- $\text{(c)}\quad\frac{15}{64} = 23.4\%$
- $\text{(d)}\quad\frac{20}{64} = 31.3\%$
- $\text{(e)}\quad\frac{20+15+6+1}{64} = \frac{42}{64} = 65.6\%$

@bottom

![Original figure](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/six_child_pascal_and_choices.svg)

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 14.
