=== layout: one-panel

# *Three Sex Problem*

@body

- What is the probability that they have exactly 3 &#945;, 2 &#946;, and 2 &#611;
- To get the probability we divide by total possibilities..

$$\binom{7}{3}\cdot\binom{4}{2}\cdot\binom{2}{2}=35\times6\times1=210$$

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 21.
Layout revised against original; selected equations authored in LaTeX and rendered as measured SVG.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Three Sex Problem*

@body

- What is the probability that they have exactly 3 &#945;, 2 &#946;, and 2 &#611;
- To get the probability we divide by total possibilities..

$$\binom{7}{3}\cdot\binom{4}{2}\cdot\binom{2}{2}=35\times6\times1=210$$

$$p=\frac{210}{3^7}=\frac{210}{2187}\approx9.6\%$$

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 22.
Layout revised against original; selected equations authored in LaTeX and rendered as measured SVG.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Three Sex Problem*

@body

- What is the probability that they have exactly 3 &#945;, 2 &#946;, and 2 &#611;
- Where does 3^7^ come from, well you can use the other formula...

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\binom{7}{3}\cdot\binom{4}{2}\cdot\binom{2}{2}
\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^3\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^2\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^2\\
&=35\times6\times1\times\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^7=\frac{210}{3^7}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$p=\frac{210}{3^7}=\frac{210}{2187}\approx9.6\%$$

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 23.
Layout revised against original; selected equations authored in LaTeX and rendered as measured SVG.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Three Sex Problem*

@body

- What is the probability that they have exactly 3 &#945;, 2 &#946;, and 2 &#611;
- The order does not matter either

$$\binom{7}{3}\cdot\binom{4}{2}\cdot\binom{2}{2}=35\times6\times1=210$$

$$\binom{7}{2}\cdot\binom{5}{2}\cdot\binom{3}{3}=21\times10\times1=210$$

$$\binom{7}{2}\cdot\binom{5}{3}\cdot\binom{2}{2}=21\times10\times1=210$$

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 24.
