=== layout: stacked-panels

# *Dihybrid Cross*

@top

- What is the probability of obtaining exactly 9, 3, 3, and 1 from only 16 offspring in a dihybrid cross?
- Just like with the sexes question we need to expand our number of choose functions&#8230;
- For clarity, I will call phenotypes\: YR, gR, Yw, and gw (rather than their genotypes)

@bottom

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\binom{n}{k_{YR}}\cdot\binom{n-k_{YR}}{k_{Yw}}\cdot
\binom{n-k_{YR}-k_{Yw}}{k_{gR}}\cdot
\binom{n-k_{YR}-k_{Yw}-k_{gR}}{k_{gw}}\\
&\qquad\cdot(p_{YR})^{k_{YR}}\cdot(p_{Yw})^{k_{Yw}}
\cdot(p_{gR})^{k_{gR}}\cdot(p_{gw})^{k_{gw}}
\end{aligned}
$$

- [YUCK!]{color=red}

@notes

Source: lect07c-prob_of_progeny_2.odp, slide 33.
Layout revised against original; selected equations authored in LaTeX and rendered as measured SVG.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Dihybrid Cross*

@body

- What is the probability of obtaining exactly 9, 3, 3, and 1 from only 16 offspring in a dihybrid cross?

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\binom{16}{9}\cdot\binom{7}{3}\cdot\binom{4}{3}\cdot\binom{1}{1}
\cdot\left(\frac{9}{16}\right)^9\cdot\left(\frac{3}{16}\right)^3
\cdot\left(\frac{3}{16}\right)^3\cdot\left(\frac{1}{16}\right)^1\\
&=(11{,}440\times35\times4\times1)\cdot(0.00564)\cdot(0.00659)
\cdot(0.00659)\cdot(0.0625)\\
&=1{,}601{,}600\times1.531\cdot10^{-8}=2.45\%
\end{aligned}
$$

![Slide image 22](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/image_022.png)

![Slide image 21](assets/source_fidelity/lect07c/image_021.png)

